#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418774} :activity/id #uuid "127d383e-0561-515b-b9bf-949bec0e816d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596addf6-8054-5598-9e84-8da605078378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418746} {:answer/id #uuid "78313721-3417-534e-bd9d-35344d3b992b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418747} {:answer/id #uuid "896cdb45-076d-5afe-9612-da56ea5a4872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418749} {:answer/id #uuid "90b1dfb5-99df-5a22-8ca3-79658b3f711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418750} {:answer/id #uuid "c979a875-601a-5ca5-ac66-79e409fb9e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418746} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bf075f-8e0c-5f11-88cc-cf746d21f00c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e54a7c62-bee3-5a5a-868c-0fd63367a8c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80525" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b+c}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b+c}=\\frac{3}{2+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0577a2b9-4489-58d5-ac62-a197003874f0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4730af-deb8-5685-8db4-054a0f26476b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418737} {:answer/id #uuid "45409e4f-d6f7-583a-a654-f6549650ec01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418740} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf16aae-72e1-5107-ae16-5b805fc64b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418742} {:answer/id #uuid "510cc82f-89b0-512e-a913-16359c5c9470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7982ef7-967e-53d6-ac5f-6caebe695c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71e16f6-f24e-58ff-9688-d083942b3ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95765da4-bd97-53db-8498-26a824409464" :question/math-academy-id "q-80524" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "35869f4e-ea24-507d-8562-2faa7b32b71c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2560798b-15eb-58b9-9b21-90ea11e0e02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418738} {:answer/id #uuid "2fd54508-dbfe-5bf5-8588-0629d075c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418739} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e698f-5a53-5e88-a9d0-bf26c7dc0622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418741} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b5d004-ff0d-52e0-ae59-f92ef8861bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418756} {:answer/id #uuid "d3984660-76a2-5389-8742-ab715d50f835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418757} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7279778-d43a-559a-840e-ae02e897e9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a115f8b-a248-5ebc-93b2-6b42bb9d7f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-140470" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}).$\n So, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"} :step/id #uuid "76d46c90-8a7d-5c38-905d-f11edade0d5a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2c0c306-a99a-558a-be4a-efd202ff8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418751} {:answer/id #uuid "a60eb3ab-afb3-5fa3-81f8-2c88c661879b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418752} {:answer/id #uuid "b53d73b5-cdbe-5038-ac9f-f388bc73961d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418753} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85a2f4-6927-58bb-a2be-eaa0257991e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418754} {:answer/id #uuid "d7996a02-da25-5836-9723-3f3bd4f67f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0664c98-aa8f-5458-ad72-e93d50a1082f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac83e377-f632-58f6-8bed-4004335968a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80514" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=-8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7791ae0e-534a-5b36-b058-c53eaf417327" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5210b26c-a0e7-5a21-a878-831f0c686a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418744} {:answer/id #uuid "541db5fa-5828-53cc-9410-f2d882f278c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418745} {:answer/id #uuid "8938062f-6293-5274-8504-3f32097a764b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418748} {:answer/id #uuid "fb610657-ef5d-596b-a8a0-847fe72dc416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418760} {:answer/id #uuid "fc792583-9e07-55f0-920d-d217098932ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e9ece4-46c5-5719-8b0a-2409c92f79a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49bf04a8-ac22-532b-bad0-4061fdca5fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39624" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{2}$ only\"."} :step/id #uuid "c995944d-1a26-5669-9909-6a019a785d1b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418773}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418736}]}
